tring, Vec<RuntimeVersionEntry>>; AC-004/AC-005 restated as single-element-array assertions; added AC-005b for the dual-manifest case (both entries coexist, npm-then-python order, NOT an error -- unlike AC-017's platform-target conflict, two runtime constraints on one package are not contradictory); AC-009 extended to cover the two-element ordering rule. (2) The map-key accessor (PackageId::name() vs display_name()) was never pinned. Resolved by verifying against crates/callisto-graph/src/walk.rs:82,123, which already establishes name() (bare name string) as this codebase's convention for package-name-keyed maps (`index.bare.insert(primary_id.name().to_string(), ...)`); pinned matrix's keys to the same accessor. Confirmed safe because every walker-discovered package carries a PackageId::Bare identity (never Prefixed -- Prefixed is exclusive to [[package]]/[[package-set]] config-rule patterns) and the walker already rejects same-bare-name collisions via GraphError::DuplicatePackage at discovery time (walk.rs:125-132), so no display_name()-vs-name() collision scenario can actually arise for a real workspace; the fix is about removing implementer ambiguity, not resolving a real collision risk. AC-006/AC-007/AC-009 updated to state --package matches against this same name() string.\n\nFixes applied from vector-challenger round 1 (spec-level architectural gap surfaced during planning, not caught by 6 rounds of canon review because it requires reading the workspace LOCATOR, not just the matrix module): (1) AC-010/AC-010b assumed matrix() could observe a malformed manifest in ANY directory, but matrix() (per this spec's own architecture) iterates only already-registered packages, and crates/callisto-graph/src/locate/ignore_walk.rs silently drops a directory from package discovery entirely when its only manifest fails to parse (`if let Ok(...)` with no error arm, verified at both the package.json and pyproject.toml read sites) -- so a directory whose SOLE manifest is malformed never becomes a registered package and matrix() never reaches it, making the original AC-010/AC-010b unimplementable as stated. Both criteria are narrowed to require the malformed manifest to sit in an already-registered package's directory (registered via a co-located valid manifest of a different kind), with the exact locator behavior cited inline so the implementer does not have to rediscover it. (2) Formally recorded matrix.rs's use of an independent read path instead of NapiTargetsIndex::load in scope item (1), with the exact two reasons (silent non-array coercion at napi.rs:54; GroupName-keyed rather than package-keyed) -- this was previously only true implicitly via 'reuse triple_to_role, not a second table' but the specific alternative to NapiTargetsIndex::load was undocumented, creating spec/implementation drift risk a future canon-verifier pass would flag."
